.TH DDCOLORD 1 2026-11-27 "ddcolord 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ddcolord \- keep colord's display profile in step with the monitor's color mode
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ddcolord
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-check | \-\-probe ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ddcolord
polls a monitor's color mode (an OSD setting such as sRGB, Adobe RGB or
Display P3) over DDC/CI and makes the matching ICC profile the default for
that display in
.BR colord (1).
Applications that use the "system display profile" from colord, such as
darktable, then follow the monitor.
.PP
Each connected output whose EDID matches a
.B [[monitor]]
entry in the config gets a colord display device with the metadata
.BI XRANDR_name= connector
(for example
.BR DP\-2 ),
which is how colord clients look a display up.
Devices and profiles are created with colord's temporary scope: they
disappear when
.B ddcolord
exits, so a stopped daemon leaves no stale profile behind.
.PP
The I2C bus is found through the connector's
.B ddc
link in sysfs where the driver provides one, and otherwise by reading the
EDID on each I2C adapter of the same GPU and comparing it with the
connector's.
On such a driver, two connected monitors on one GPU with byte\-identical
EDIDs cannot be told apart, and both are reported as an error instead of
guessed.
The bus is locked with
.BR flock (2)
around each exchange, the same lock
.BR ddcutil (1)
takes, so the two do not interleave; a bus locked by someone else is skipped
until the next poll.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIpath\fR"
Config file.
Default:
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ddcolord/config.toml ,
or
.I ~/.config/ddcolord/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or not absolute.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Load the config, check that it lists at least one monitor and that every
ICC file it names is readable and carries the ICC signature, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-probe
For each connected output, print its EDID vendor, model and serial (in the
form the config wants), the I2C bus, the raw value of the color mode
feature, and what the config maps it to.
Works without a config.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Log every poll.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter, overriding the level chosen by
.BR \-\-debug .
.TP
.B NOTIFY_SOCKET
Set by systemd; readiness is reported once the config is loaded and the
system bus is connected.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 for
.BR \-\-check " and " \-\-probe
when they succeed, 1 on any error, 2 for a usage error.
The daemon exits 1 when it loses the system bus, since its colord devices
went with the connection.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/i2c\-*
Read and written for DDC/CI; the user needs access (on most distributions,
membership in group
.BR i2c ).
.SH EXAMPLES
Find what to put in the config:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ddcolord \-\-probe
.fi
.RE
.PP
See which profile colord hands out:
.PP
.RS
.nf
colormgr get\-devices
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ddcolord.toml (5),
.BR colormgr (1),
.BR ddcutil (1)
